+- `figures/samples.pdf` (Figure 1) — seed-0 samples of the `none` and `low` arms beside a real
+  validation graph, with edge and triangle counts computed at draw time. `high` and `random` are
+  scored and their triangle counts printed to stdout, but not drawn: see `SAMPLE_PANELS` in the
+  script, and §4.1 for where those two numbers are quoted.
+- `figures/sweep.pdf` (Figure 2) — the *k*-sweep in a frequency view and an information view.
+
+Neither contains a hand-typed number, and the script raises rather than skipping if a source file
+is missing. It also still emits `figures/downstream.pdf`; that figure is cut from the paper for
+space (its numbers are stated in full in §4.6) and can be reinstated with a `\begin{figure}` block
+if you free up a column.
+
+Figure 1 needs `torch` locally, only to `torch.load` the committed sample tensors and to
+reproduce the dataset split's `torch.randperm`. A CPU wheel is enough:
+`pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cpu`.
+
+Two properties of Figure 1 are deliberate and stated in its caption, because they are what stop
+it being a cherry-picked panel: the sample shown is **index 0** of every arm, and all panels
+share one Kamada--Kawai layout, so the real graph is not drawn with the 2D coordinates its
+Delaunay triangulation actually came from.
 
